    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d510">

                <pb facs="0537" n="533" sameAs="frus:frus1939v03/pg533"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">894.24/652a: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Ambassador in Japan</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">Grew</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1939-04-27T17:00:00-05:00">April 27, 1939—5
                            p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>113. Press flashes this noon state that Senator Pittman has introduced a
                        bill<anchor n="4" xml:id="d510-f1"/> which would authorize the President to
                    place restrictions upon trade and commerce between the United States and any
                    violator of the Nine Power Treaty of 1922; also that the Senator said that his
                    plan was drafted “By me alone, and no one else knows anything about it.”</p>
                <p>At his press conference today, the Secretary, when asked for comment, said that
                    he had no knowledge of the matter until he saw the news flash; that he had not
                    had any conference on the subject; and that he thought that Senator Pittman had
                    made it clear in the statement which accompanied his introduction of the bill
                    that the bill was his own individual product.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
